When indefinite pronouns like somebody, anybody, everyone, something, etc. are followed by "else," the possessive apostrophe ('s) attaches to "else" — not to the pronoun itself.

The Rule

Pronoun + else's (not pronoun's + else)

Examples

(a) Somebody's purse has been stolen.

No "else" here, so possessive goes directly on "somebody" — normal rule: somebody's

(b) ❌ Somebody's else's purse has been stolen.
✅ Somebody else's purse has been stolen.

Since "else" is added, the apostrophe moves to after "else," not after "somebody."
"Somebody" stays plain (no apostrophe), and "else's" carries the possession.


"Ram's uncle's friend" — Chained Possessive

When there's a chain of possession (one person possessing something that possesses something else), each link in the chain gets its own apostrophe + 's.

Breaking it down:

Ram's uncle's friend
= the friend of Ram's uncle
= Ram has an uncle → that uncle has a friend

Ram's → shows Ram possesses the uncle (Ram's uncle)
uncle's → shows the uncle possesses the friend (uncle's friend)
Each possessive link needs its own 's, not just one at the end.


More examples of chained possession:
Sita's sister's house (the house belonging to Sita's sister)
My friend's brother's car (the car belonging to my friend's brother)
India's Prime Minister's speech (the speech of India's Prime Minister)
Quick rule to remember:

In a possessive chain, every noun in the chain that "owns" the next one needs its own apostrophe + 's — you can't skip any link.
